
  [1mShort design[0m

  [1mThe real choice.[0m Pure word-boundary isn't actually viable on its own: [38;5;153mformat("supercalifragilistic", { truncate: 10 })
[39m  has no space in the budget, so backing off to the last boundary yields an empty string. Any word-boundary version
  needs a hard-cut fallback. So the choice is between hard cut, and word-boundary-with-fallback — which is hard cut plus
  about three lines.

  - [1mA — Hard cut.[0m [38;5;153mstr.slice(0, max - 3) + '...'[39m. Shortest, fully predictable, cuts mid-word: [38;5;153m"hello world"[39m at 9 → [38;5;153m"hello
[39m    [38;5;153m..."[39m.
  - [1mB — Word boundary, hard-cut fallback.[0m Take the same [38;5;153mmax - 3[39m budget, back off to the last whitespace, trim trailing
    space, and fall back to the hard cut when no boundary exists. [38;5;153m"hello world"[39m at 9 → [38;5;153m"hello..."[39m.

  [1mI recommend B.[0m You already named the reason — it's the better read — and the fallback that makes it correct is also
  what makes the cost negligible. It's strictly A with a [38;5;153mlastIndexOf[39m and a guard, same complexity class, no new failure
  mode: when there's no boundary it [3mis[0m A.

  [1mBehavior I'm pinning down[0m (say if any is wrong):

  - [38;5;153mstr.length <= maxLength[39m → returned unchanged, no ellipsis appended.
  - [38;5;153mmaxLength <= 3[39m → returns [38;5;153m'...'.slice(0, maxLength)[39m, so the never-exceed invariant holds even at [38;5;153mmaxLength: 1[39m.
  - Trailing whitespace is trimmed before the ellipsis, so you get [38;5;153m"hello..."[39m, not [38;5;153m"hello ..."[39m.
  - [1mOrder:[0m truncate runs after the case transforms and before [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m, so [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m bounds the string being
    truncated and caller-supplied chrome always survives. Consequence: with a prefix, the final return value can exceed
    [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m. The alternative — truncating last so [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m bounds the whole return — lets a long prefix eat all the
    content. This one is cheap to flip later if you'd rather have the hard ceiling on the final string.
  - [1mOption shape:[0m [38;5;153mformat(str, { truncate: 20 })[39m — a plain number, matching the existing flat-option style. It inherits
    the file's truthiness guard, so [38;5;153mtruncate: 0[39m reads as "no truncation" rather than "truncate to nothing".

